===Noun===
{{en-noun|s|fistulae|fistulæ}}

# {{lb|en|medicine}} An [[abnormal]] [[connection]] or passageway between [[organ]]s or [[vessel]]s that normally do not connect.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1903|author=William Rice Pryor|title=Gynæcology|page=113|text=Small '''fistulæ''' are to be closed bilaterally in an antero-posterior line{{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1917|author=Louis Adolph Merillat|title=Fistula of the Withers and Poll-Evil|page=5|text=There are several reasons why a manual on this disease should be a part of the veterinary literature of the day, the chief one being that '''fistula''' of the withers is a very prevalent disease of horses and thus exacts a big toll from the horse industry.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1998|author=Scott Fisher|chapter=Enterocutaneous '''Fistulas'''|editor=Theodore J. Saclarides; Keith W. Millikan|title=Common Surgical Diseases: An Algorithmic Approach to Problem Solving|url=http://books.google.com/books?id=Wh_fXY2Bo38C&pg=PA164&dq=%22Enterocutaneous+fistulas%22&hl=en&ei=T9s7TpbTKaPFmAWnyLGEAw&sa=X&oi=book_result&ct=result&resnum=4&ved=0CDYQ6AEwAw#v=onepage&q=%22Enterocutaneous%20fistulas%22&f=false|page=164|text='''Fistulas''' are abnormal communications between two epithelialized surfaces. The causes of enterocutaneous '''fistulas''' can be remembered using the mnemonic FRIEND: '''F'''oreign body, '''R'''adiation, '''I'''nflammation/Infection/Inflammatory bowel disease, '''E'''pithelialization, '''N'''eoplasm, and '''D'''istal obstruction. Fifteen to twenty-five percent of enterocutaneous '''fistulas''' arise spontaneously as in, for example, Crohn's disease or cancer.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2008|author=Sylvia Escott-Stump|title=Nutrition and Diagnosis-related Care|page=405|text=An intestinal '''fistula''' is an unwanted pathway from intestines to other organs (e.g., the bladder).}}
# {{lb|en|rare}} A [[tube]], a [[pipe]], or a [[hole]].
# {{lb|en|Christianity|historical}} The tube through which the [[wine]] of the [[Eucharist]] was once sucked from the [[chalice]] in certain ceremonies (such as [[papal]] [[Mass]]es).
#: {{syn|en|calamus}}

====Usage notes====
* {{sense|medicine}} The skin is regarded as an organ, so the definition includes the abnormal connection of an internal organ to the body's exterior (as in, for example, enterocutaneous fistulas).
* Sometimes, a fistula will be intentionally created; for example, an [[w:Arteriovenous fistula|arteriovenous fistula]] is sometimes created to ease the treatment of a patient with end stage renal failure.

